  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale:\n\n   Central Idea:\n   We adopt a coordinate descent approach that alternates between optimizing the positions of similarly weighted pulses and their widths, constrained by a smoothness penalty. This differs from the previous program's multi-stage optimization by explicitly separating position-dependent and broadness factors.\n\n   Problem Insight:\n   The optimal solution likely exhibits localization and periodic structure. Our model decomposes the step function into narrow pulses separated by fixed distances, capturing the periodic pattern while allowing finer tuning through adaptive width estimation.\n\n   Bottlenecks Addressed:\n   - Previous methods failed to capture the preferred periodicity\n   - Optimal width and position are interdependent but funda-mentally different scales\n   - Early local optimizations missed global structure\n\n   Important Limitations:\n   - Smoothness parameter \u03b1 requires careful tuning\n   - Convergence isn't guaranteed to global optimum\n   - Number of pulses is fixed for computational feasibility\n\n2. Mathematical Specification:\n\n   Representation:\n   h(x) = (\u03b2 / N) * \u03c3\u2098 exp(-\u03b1 |x - x_i - k*2/3|), with \u03b2 control parameter\n\n   Variables:\n   - m pulse centers (x_i) \u2208 [0,2]\n   - Decay parameter \u03b1\n   - Number of basis functions N\n   - Overall intensity \u03b2\n\n   Exact Objective:\n\n   min max\u2096 \u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx\n   subject to: \u222bh(x)dx = 1/2\n\n   Formulation:\n   We use a log-sum-exp (LSE) surrogate built from the correlation signal. Let c(\u03b1,N) = max\u2096 correlation(\u03c8, 1-\u03c8) where \u03c8 represents the current pulse configuration.\n\n   Implementation:\n   - Objective: log(\u2211_j exp(-\u03b1|c_j - m|/\u03b2)) + m (penalty)\n   - Constraints: \u03b1 \u2208 [\u03b1_min, \u03b1_max], N \u2208 {50,100,150,200}\n   - Transform integral constraint to \u03b2 selection: \u03b2 = 0.5 / (\u2211 \u03b1_exp(i xxix))\n\n   3. Implementation Procedure:\n\n   Pseudocode Controller:\n\n   ```python\nimport numpy as np\nfrom scipy.optimize import minimize\n\ndef build_h(x_center, alpha, beta, N):\n    \"\"\"Build pulse-specific activation function\"\"\"\n    x = np.linspace(0, 2, 1000)\n    h_vals = np.zeros(len(x))\n    for center in x_center:\n        shift = (center % 2)\n        for k in range(-int(N/2), int(N/2)+1):\n            if k == 0:\n                kernel = np.exp(-alpha*np.abs(x - shift))\n            else:\n                kernel = np.exp(-alpha*np.abs(x - shift - 2*np.pi*k/3)) * k**2\n            h_vals += beta * kernel\n    return h_vals\n\ndef eval_objective(params, N):\n    \"\"\"Computes the LSE objective\"\"\"\n    alpha, beta, dtype(N)] coordinates are set from average centers based on N value}})\ndtypes = {\n    50: [float, float, 30],\n    100: [float, float, 50],\n    150: [float, float, 60],\n    200: [float, float, 75]\n}\n\nopt_params_base = [3.5, 0.5]  # [alpha_init, beta_init]\n\nfor N in [50, 100, 150, 200, 250]:\n    d = dtypes[N]\n    bounds = [\n        [alpha_lower, alpha_upper],\n        [beta_lower, beta_upper]\n    ]\n    try:\n        res = minimize(lambda p: lse_surrogate(p[0], p[1], N) \n                  + penalty_function(p), \n                  opt_params_base + [N], \n                  bounds=bounds,\n                  method='L-BFGS-B')\n    except:\n        continue\n\n   ```\n\n3. Implementation Procedure Details:\n\n   Detailed Flow:\n\n   A. Initialization:\n      - Set up grid of pulse centers uniformly spaced every 2/3\n      - Calculate initial integral constraint using \u03b2 optimization\n      - Set iterative bounds for \u03b1 and \u03b2\n\n   B. Coordinate Descent Loop:\n\n      1. Position Optimization:\n          \u2022 Fix \u03b2, N\n          \u2022 Use L-BFGS-B to minimize correlation over pulse positions\n          \u2022 Add regularization on position spacing\n          \u2022 Track current grid correlation score\n\n      2. Width Optimization:\n          \u2022 Fix pulse positions (from last iteration)\n          \u2022 Optimize \u03b1 and both \u03b2 values simultaneously\n          \u2022 Incorporate boundary regularization\n\n   C. Local Refinement:\n      \u2022 Multi-grid refinement: project current solution to fewer grid points\n      \u2022 Increase pulse separation for each refinement level\n      \u2022 Keep periodic across all grid levels\n\n   D. Resampling Strategy:\n      \u2022 Only change period multipliers when stuck in local minima\n      \u2022 Allow random walk when correlation improvements plateau\n      \u2022 Maintain separate cache of top 5 configurations per refinement level\n\n   E. Termination:\n      \u2022 Track lowest correlation found and iteration count\n      \u2022 Use gradient magnitude as success indicator\n      \u2022 Time-based early stopping after 100 iterations\n      \u2022 Compare to Erd\u0151s constant tuning parameter\n\n   4. Search Choices and Variations:\n\n   Parameter Settings:\n\n   - Base parameters: \u03b1 \u2208 [1.0,5.0], \u03b2 \u2208 [0.2,0.8]\n   - Periods: 30,50,60,75 points\n   - Smoothness: L2 regularization coefficient (\u03ba_min=1.0, \u03ba_max=10.0)\n   - Iteration damping: initial learning rate 0.1 (schedule: adaptive)\n\n   Key Variations:\n\n   1. Power Law Exponents:\n      \u2022 Property: dynamically adaptive width estimation\n      \u2022 Use log-normal distribution base for neighborhood weighting\n      \u2022 Choice triggered by detection of correlation plateaus\n\n   2. Multi-scale Optimization:\n      \u2022 Grid-resolution parameter N \u2208 {30,50,70,90}\n      \u2022 Higher N provides finer detail but increases compute cost\n      \u2022 Schedule: start with low N, increase by factor 2 on improvement\n\n   3. Stochastic Optimization:\n      \u2022 Randomized baseline spacing check\n      \u2022 Random restarts on a grid around best candidate\n      \u2022 Progress: only valid for integer N values\n\n   4. Progressive Tangent Refinement:\n      \u2022 Multiple correlation checks per iteration\n      \u2022 Full numerical gradient vs central difference\n      \u2022 Valid only for positive definite searching domains\n\n   5. Computation Budget:\n\n   Time Estimation:\n   - High-level: assume ~500 iterations on 6 cores\n   - Critical components: correlation evaluation (dense)\n   - Nested checks: avoid CPU-bound failures\n\n   The coordinate descent approach systematically explores the relationship between pulse positions and widths, with periodicity enforced through the 2/3-separated grid optimization. The method is self-contained and doesn't rely on parent solutions, using only the initialization parameters provided.",
